      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey quick question... im seeing these tiny little holes appearing all over my tomato leaves in the greenhouse. they're really small and hard to spot unless u look closely. i cant see any bugs actually crawling on them though. any idea what might be doing that??",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Those tiny holes on your tomato leaves can be quite a nuisance, can't they? It's not uncommon for pests to leave behind such subtle signs of their presence, rather like a mischievous band of goblins leaving tiny footprints in the dust. You see, these holes are likely caused by aphid eggs, which can be incredibly small and hard to spot, especially if you're not looking closely enough. It's a bit like searching for a single grain of sand on a vast beach, but with a keen eye, you might just spot the telltale signs of aphid activity. \n\nNow, I'm not saying that aphids are the only culprits, but they're a common cause of such damage. Another possibility is spider mites, which can also leave behind tiny holes in the leaves. But, just like a goblin's lair, these pests often leave behind a trail of subtle clues that can help you track them down. \n\nTo get to the bottom of this mystery, I'd recommend taking a closer look at the affected leaves and searching for any signs of aphid or spider mite activity. You might also want to consider introducing some natural predators, like ladybugs or lacewings, to help",
